.Dd September 21, 2026
.Dt PG_LASWELL_CITUS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pg_laswell_citus
.Nd Citus support in pg_laswell: intent kinds, readings and refusals
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Citus support is a
.Em build-time module .
The core binary speaks PostgreSQL and nothing else; a binary that carries this
module additionally knows the kinds below, takes an extra reading of the
cluster's topology, and refuses several things a stock build would accept and
then fail to execute.
.Pp
This page describes a binary built with the module.
To tell whether you have one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pg_laswell_mcp --version
0.1.2
modules: citus
.Ed
.Pp
A binary with no
.Li modules
line does not recognise any kind on this page, and will refuse a specification
that names one -- the whole specification, not the one intent, which is the same
rule that governs any unknown kind.
Build one with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cmake -S cpp -B build -DPGLASWELL_MODULES=citus
.Ed
.Pp
Every kind here is prefixed
.Cm citus_ ,
so a specification says which vendor it needs and two vendors cannot collide on
a name.
The
.Em MODULES
section of
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
gives the rules a module works under, and what it is forbidden to do.
.Sh INTENT KINDS
.\" BEGIN CITUS KIND LIST -- checked by tools/check-manual.py against
.\" cpp/src/modules/citus/kinds.inc.
.Ss Cm citus_distribute_table
Hash-distribute a table across the cluster's worker nodes.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table ,
.Va distribution_column ,
.Va comment .
Optional:
.Va shard_method ,
.Va colocate_with ,
.Va shard_count ,
.Va concurrently .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va distribution_column
The column whose hash picks the shard.
.Em Every
unique constraint and primary key on the table must include it: Citus cannot
enforce uniqueness across shards without it, so it refuses the distribution
rather than the constraint.
An intent whose table has a key not covering this column is refused, naming the
key and the columns it does cover.
.It Va shard_method
.Cm hash
(the default),
.Cm append
or
.Cm range .
Hash is the only form Citus recommends for new work; the other two are legacy.
.It Va colocate_with
.Qq schema.table ,
or one of Citus's own words
.Cm none
and
.Cm default .
An unqualified name is refused as ambiguous with those sentinels, so a table
genuinely called
.Li none
is written
.Qq public.none .
Colocating requires the named table to be distributed already -- in an earlier
intent of the same specification, which is accounted for, or an earlier
specification ordered by
.Va depends_on .
.It Va shard_count
A positive integer.
Mutually exclusive with a real
.Va colocate_with :
joining a colocation group adopts that group's shard count, so naming both asks
for two different numbers.
Refused at parse time rather than half-applied at execution.
.It Va concurrently
.Cm auto
(the default),
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
Under
.Cm auto
the planner chooses from the table's estimated size and records which reading
decided.
.Cm always
requires
.Fn create_distributed_table_concurrently ,
which needs a replica identity -- a table with no primary key cannot use it --
and is refused, not silently downgraded, on a Citus too old to have it.
Someone who wrote
.Cm always
was making a decision about a table too big to lock, and quietly locking it
anyway is the worst available answer.
.El
.Ss Cm citus_create_reference_table
Replicate a table in full to every node, so reads join against it locally.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va table ,
.Va comment .
No other keys are accepted.
.Pp
Always warns: every write to a reference table becomes a two-phase commit across
all nodes.
Reference tables are for small, rarely written lookups; a busy one is a
cluster-wide bottleneck.
.Ss Cm citus_distribute_function
Route calls of a function to the node holding the relevant shard, or replicate
its definition to every node.
.Pp
Required:
.Va schema ,
.Va name ,
.Va arguments ,
.Va comment .
Optional:
.Va distribution_argument ,
.Va colocate_with ,
.Va force_delegation .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va arguments
The argument type list that identifies the overload, as PostgreSQL writes it.
.It Va distribution_argument Ns / Ns Va colocate_with
These travel together and either both appear or neither does.
The argument says which parameter routes the call; the colocation target says
which table's shards it routes to.
One without the other cannot be acted on, and is refused.
.It Va force_delegation
Boolean.
.El
.Pp
With neither colocation key, the function is replicated to every node rather
than routed, and the plan warns: every node gets a copy of the definition, and a
later
.Ic CREATE OR REPLACE
has to be propagated too or the copies diverge.
.Pp
The function must already exist; create it in an earlier specification and order
the two with
.Va depends_on .
.\" END CITUS KIND LIST
.Sh READINGS
With the module built in, every plan against a database where the
.Li citus
extension is installed carries an extra reading under
.Va extensions.citus :
the extension version, the worker count, which distributed tables exist with
their distribution column and colocation group, which functions are distributed,
whether this Citus has
.Fn create_distributed_table_concurrently ,
and the values of
.Va citus.enable_ddl_propagation
and
.Va citus.max_adaptive_executor_pool_size .
.Pp
An absent reading is not a zero reading.
A database with no Citus installed produces no
.Va extensions.citus
at all, and every kind on this page is refused there with that as the reason,
rather than planned against defaults.
.Pp
Contention is read differently too.
On a coordinator,
.Va pg_locks
shows the coordinator's own waits and not a worker's, so a shard-level wait is
invisible where a single-node reading would find it.
Where the module applies, waiters come from
.Va citus_lock_waits
and activity from
.Va citus_stat_activity ,
which see the whole cluster.
.Sh REFUSALS
These are the reason the module exists: each is a Citus error that would
otherwise surface at execution, partway through a migration, on a cluster, with
earlier work already committed.
All of them are derivable from the readings above beforehand.
.Ss Topology-wide
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va citus.enable_ddl_propagation Li is off
Refused for
.Em every
kind, core kinds included -- a plain
.Cm create_table
on a Citus cluster is propagated DDL too.
With propagation off, DDL runs on the coordinator and not on the workers, and
the two catalogs diverge
.Em silently :
every reading pg_laswell takes, including the check that catches drift, is taken
on the coordinator.
The ledger would record the migration as fully applied, the coordinator would
agree, and the shards would not have it.
A warning would be a false reassurance; refusing is recoverable in one
statement.
.It A paced walk against a distributed table
.Cm backfill ,
.Cm update_rows ,
.Cm delete_rows
and
.Cm merge_rows
pace themselves by taking each batch
.Ic FOR UPDATE ,
which is what stops a keyset cursor selecting the same rows twice.
Citus rejects that on a multi-shard query:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ERROR: could not run distributed query with FOR UPDATE/SHARE commands
.Ed
.Pp
So this does not merely perform badly -- it does not run at all, and without the
refusal it would be accepted here and fail partway through.
Reference tables are exempt: one placement per node is not a multi-shard query.
Local tables on a Citus cluster are unaffected.
.Pp
Liftable, and intended to be: confining a batch to one shard makes
.Ic FOR UPDATE
legal again.
Until then this is the honest answer.
.El
.Ss Per kind
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
The
.Li citus
extension is not installed in this database.
.It
A unique constraint or primary key does not include the distribution column.
.It
The distribution column does not exist on the table.
.It
.Va colocate_with
names a table this database does not record as distributed.
.It
.Va concurrently : Cm always
was asked for and this Citus has no concurrent form.
.It
.Va concurrently : Cm always
on a table with no replica identity.
.It
A function named for distribution does not exist.
.It
.Va shard_count
together with a real
.Va colocate_with ,
or
.Va distribution_argument
without
.Va colocate_with .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Distribute a parent, then colocate a child with it, in one specification.
The second intent is planned against the state the first will have produced, so
it does not need a separate deployment:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "laswell_spec_version": 1,
  "id": "0030-distribute",
  "description": "Distribute accounts and colocate its ledger.",
  "intents": [
    { "kind": "citus_distribute_table",
      "schema": "public", "table": "accounts",
      "distribution_column": "id",
      "comment": "Hash distributed on id." },
    { "kind": "citus_distribute_table",
      "schema": "public", "table": "account_transactions",
      "distribution_column": "account_id",
      "colocate_with": "public.accounts",
      "comment": "Colocated with accounts." }
  ]
}
.Ed
.Pp
A runnable three-node cluster and the specifications above are in
.Pa examples/docker/citus
in the source.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pg_laswell 1 ,
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
.Pp
.Xr pg_laswell_mcp 1
is the authoritative reference for the specification format, pacing, trust and
the ledger; this page covers only what the Citus module adds.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Daniel Cristian Cruz
.Sh CAVEATS
The module set is not hashed into
.Va planDigest .
For a specification a binary can plan at all the module set does not change a
single statement, and one it cannot plan is refused outright -- so hashing it
would change every existing digest while protecting nothing.
.Pp
Distributing a table is not reversible by re-running a specification.
Retiring one means
.Fn undistribute_table
by hand, which is outside what a signed specification describes.
